    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "В этом мире крысы — враги.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'In this world rats are enemies.' Russian leaves out 'am', 'is', or 'are' in the present tense, linking 'that' straight to 'enemy'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Их все очень ненавидят.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Everyone hates them very much.' The character links 'them' directly with 'all' to express the thought clearly.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "И вы тоже крыс не любите.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'And you also do not like rats.' The character combines 'and' with 'to love' in a natural Russian turn of phrase.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Мыши очень похожи на них.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Mice are very similar to them.' Notice how 'mouse' sets up the clause and leads naturally into 'them'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Да, я их не люблю.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Yes, I do not like them.' Chef Viktor links 'yes' directly with 'I' to express the thought clearly.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Они как милые мыши.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'They are like cute mice.' The character combines 'they' with 'mouse' in a natural Russian turn of phrase.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Но крыс все ненавидят.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'But everyone hates rats.' This everyday Russian sentence pairs 'but' with 'hate'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Извините, мне звонят.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Excuse me, they are calling me.' The character uses the imperative command form of the verb meaning 'sorry'.",
